//? Title: ZFNet: Finer Sampling and Deconvnet Feature Projections
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Large early strides can discard image detail before a network has learned useful features.
ZFNet revises early sampling and uses deconvnet projections to inspect hidden responses. We
verify its pooling switches and backward sign rule, train the complete compact topology with
visible tensor Adam updates, and compare one feature projection with its ordinary input gradient.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.2_classic_architectures/zfnet.cpp`.

> The method

This Figure-3 variant uses dense channel connectivity throughout. A one-pixel spatial pad
precedes a VALID 7×7 stride-two stem; SAME max-pooling gives the reference 224→110→55
sizes. VALID 5×5 stride-two conv2 and SAME pooling give 26→13. Three SAME 3×3 convolutions
preserve 13, then VALID pooling gives 6. The widths are 96/256/384/384/256; two 4096-unit
ReLU layers and 1000 logits give 62357608 learned scalars. LRN follows the first two pools.
This differs from AlexNet's grouped layers and 11×11 stride-four stem. He/Glorot initializers
and inverted .5 dropout are explicit adaptations. A dense convolution costs O(NHoWo k² Cin Cout).

Deconvnet sends an activation signal back through pooling switches and convolution adjoints.
If s is a backward signal and W the forward kernel, each convolutional step is

$$
s_{previous}=W^{T}\operatorname{ReLU}(s).
$$
\alttext(Rectify the reconstructed signal before applying the learned convolution adjoint.)

At pooling, the signal returns to each window's forward argmax; overlapping contributions add.
The convolution transpose uses the exact original input shape, including stride remainders.
This rule gates on the sign of s. Ordinary ReLU differentiation gates on the forward activation.
For forward (-1,2) and backward (3,-4), their outputs are respectively (3,0) and (0,-4).
Guided backprop would gate on both signs and give (0,0) for this fixture. Deconvnet omits LRN
inversion and bias subtraction; it is not a mathematical inverse or the derivative of a class score.
The local switch VJP intentionally detaches its inputs, so this diagnostic is not a trainable layer.

The dataset fit keeps every stage at quarter widths, with two 256-unit dense layers and 67-pixel
bilinear input resizing. It uses 128 fitting, 64 validation and 64 official-test rows, 20 epochs,
batch eight, Adam(.001), explicit kernel L2=.0001/2, independent crop/flip augmentation and .5
dropout. Validation NLL selects a saved checkpoint. A separate block reloads it and projects
the strongest conv5 feature of the first test image using a unit signal, then compares ordinary
differentiation with the same feature seed. Cached CIFAR-10 is required.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/zfnet_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/zfnet_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/zfnet_concept.tikz > build/zfnet_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/zfnet_concept.svg build/zfnet_concept.dvi > build/zfnet_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
