\caption(Selected-checkpoint diagnostic for one feature and one test image. Each heatmap uses its own colour range. Deconvnet omits LRN and gates the backward signal; ordinary differentiation includes the forward graph.)
\alttext(Selected-checkpoint diagnostic for one feature and one test image. Each heatmap uses its own colour range. Deconvnet omits LRN and gates the backward signal; ordinary differentiation includes the forward graph.)


The checkpoint selected at epoch 16 has inference training NLL 2.004603,
validation NLL 2.182336 and test-subset NLL 2.200640, with 21.875% accuracy.
The selected epoch differs from the final epoch; the test images do not choose it.
These small, separately fitted subsets do not establish a model ranking.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/zfnet_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

Finer early sampling changes where the network retains spatial detail. Deconvnet uses learned
filters and forward pooling switches to show one hidden response, while ordinary differentiation
answers a different local sensitivity question. The small CIFAR-10 fit and single-feature maps
demonstrate those operations without claiming an ImageNet result or a complete explanation.

> References

- [Zeiler and Fergus (2014), Visualizing and Understanding Convolutional Networks](https://cs.nyu.edu/~fergus/papers/zeilerECCV2014.pdf).
- [CIFAR-10 dataset and binary layout](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html).
